# Licence

Flamme Retardé is not one work under one licence. It is a program that draws a
real place, and the place came from somewhere. The code is mine, and so is the
sound of the place now; the terrain, the streets and the buildings are other
people's, on terms I have to pass along intact. This file says which is which.

    src/, tools/, build.py ................. MIT
    Everything else I made ................. CC BY-SA 4.0
    Three.js r0.180.0 ...................... MIT
    OpenStreetMap-derived payload .......... ODbL 1.0 (+ attribution)
    Terrain heightfield .................... public domain
    build/payload/panel_*.webp,
      refs/panels/*.png .................... see "The intro panels", below

If you only want the short version: take the engine and do what you like with
it; if you ship the *game*, or something built out of it, keep it open on the
same terms and carry the credits.

---

## 1. The code — MIT

Applies to `src/`, `tools/`, `build.py`, and the build machinery in `src/shell.html`
and `src/styles.css`. These are the parts worth stealing — the terrain sampler,
the propBuilder winding, the ground-collision solver, the synthesised audio
graph — and they are yours with no strings beyond the notice.

Where a source file carries writing rather than machinery, the writing is section
2's and the code around it is section 1's. `src/02-i18n.js` is the clear case: the
lookup is MIT, the three languages of prose inside it are CC BY-SA. Lift the
mechanism freely; quote the script and you are attributing.

