## 2. The game — CC BY-SA 4.0

Copyright (c) 2026 unk1911, licensed under the
Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International licence.
Full text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode

Applies to the authored content rather than the machinery:

- the prose — `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, the intro narration and every string
  in `src/02-i18n.js`, in all three languages;
- the hand-built worlds — the Jadrija layout, Aerodrom Rokići, the mission, and
  the placement of everything in them;
- the Blender models and their sources: `build/landmarks.blend`,
  `build/firefighter.blend`, `tools/blender/*.py`, and the baked
  `build/payload/*.fr3d.gz` (the cathedral, both forts, the Jadrija lighthouse,
  the Šibenik bridge, the Canadair, the firefighter);
- the field recordings and the clips cut from them —
  `build/payload/{shore,cicadas,wood,lapping,boat}.mp3`, recorded at Jadrija and
  in the channel off Šibenik and trimmed, filtered and levelled by
  `tools/cut_field.py`, and
  `build/payload/{cuk,dove}.mp3` — a scops owl and a collared dove recorded by
  the same hand;
  `build/payload/beads.mp3` is *not* one of these — see section 3, and neither
  is `build/payload/radio.mp3`, which is my recording of somebody else's
  broadcast, nor the seven bird calls
  `build/payload/{crow,gull,swift,beeeater,blackbird,swallow,wagtail}.mp3`,
  which are other people's recordings — see section 3 for all of them;
- `docs/*.png`;
- the assembled `flamme-retarde.html` as a whole.

Share-alike here is deliberate and not merely inherited. Nothing in the bundle
forces it — the ambience is my own field recording bar one CC0 sample, one
off-air one and seven borrowed bird calls; the birds are the first thing in the
bundle whose licence would have asked for share-alike anyway — and it stays
because it was already the right answer for a thing built almost entirely
out of open data and other people's generosity, and because I would rather not
argue about whether a base64 blob inside an HTML file is a Collection or an
Adaptation.

**Attribution.** Credit *Flamme Retardé* by unk1911, link to
https://github.com/unk1911/flamme-retarde, indicate changes, and keep the
third-party notices in section 3.

Note that the *facts* are not mine to license and I claim nothing over them.
The Rokići fire of 6 August 2024, the *zvončići*, the casualty figures, the
shape of the coast — those belong to the record and to the people they happened
to. Section 2 covers how I wrote them down, not what happened.

## 3. Third-party components

These carry their own terms, which override sections 1 and 2 for the files
named. All of them are redistributed here in compliance with those terms; none
of them are mine to relicense.

### Three.js r0.180.0 — MIT

Copyright © 2010-2025 three.js authors. https://threejs.org

Inlined into `flamme-retarde.html` by `build.py`, which stamps the MIT notice
into the generated file above the bundled source. `vendor/` holds the download
cache and is not committed.

### OpenStreetMap — ODbL 1.0

© OpenStreetMap contributors. https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright

Coastline, land cover, building footprints, roads, railways, place names and
landmark positions come from OSM by way of `tools/fetch_osm.py` and
`tools/bake.py`. The extracted, reprojected results are derivative databases and
stay under the ODbL:

    build/payload/town.json.gz      building footprints
    build/payload/roads.json.gz     road network
    build/payload/rail.json.gz      railway
    build/payload/places.json       place names and positions

`build/payload/terrain_c.png` is a land-cover raster rendered from the same
data, and the running game is likewise a Produced Work: those may be under the
licence in section 2, but only while the attribution above travels with them.
It appears in `README.md`, in this file, and on the title screen, where the
credit line reads *"terrain & town derived from OpenStreetMap & public elevation
data"* (`veil.credit` in `src/02-i18n.js`).

### The bead curtain — CC0 1.0

`build/payload/beads.mp3` is Freesound sound #817911, dedicated to the public
domain under Creative Commons Zero 1.0. https://freesound.org/s/817911/

CC0 waives everything, including the attribution CC BY would have required, and
that is the reason it was chosen over better-sounding recordings under other
terms: this game ships as one HTML file with nowhere to put a credit line, and
a licence that needs one does not fit it. The notice is here because saying
where a thing came from is a different obligation from being made to.

**That last argument no longer holds, and it was wrong when it was written.**
The title screen has carried a credit footer since the first build — it is
where OpenStreetMap is acknowledged — and a footer that can hold one credit can
hold another. What was really true is that nothing had yet been worth taking on
an attribution obligation for. In August 2026 seven bird recordings were, the
footer got a second line, and the sentence above is left standing as what was
believed at the time rather than quietly corrected. See *The birds* below.

Downsampled to 24 kHz mono and tapered to 1.15 s to sit with the field
recordings. It is played only for the *crossing* of the kabina's curtain; the
tail after you are through is synthesised in `src/80-audio.js`, because it has
to follow how hard the strands are still swinging.

### The birds — CC BY-SA 4.0, CC BY-SA 3.0, CC BY 4.0 and one public domain

`build/payload/{crow,gull,swift,beeeater,blackbird,swallow,wagtail}.mp3` are
cut from seven recordings on Wikimedia Commons by `tools/cut_birds.py`, which
fetches each from Commons the first time it is run and does not commit the
sources. The eighth bird, `build/payload/dove.mp3`, is mine and is covered by
section 2.

| clip | species | Commons file | recordist | licence |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `crow.mp3` | *Corvus cornix* | `Corvus cornix.ogg` | Oona Räisänen (Mysid) | public domain |
| `gull.mp3` | *Larus michahellis* | `Yellow-legged Gull - Larus michahellis michahellis.ogg` | Cedric Mroczko | CC BY-SA 4.0 |
| `swift.mp3` | *Apus pallidus* | `Cri de martinet pâle, Espagne.wav` | Xavier Riera | CC BY-SA 4.0 |
| `beeeater.mp3` | *Merops apiaster* | `Bijeneter - SoundCloud - Beeld en Geluid.ogg` | Beeld en Geluid | CC BY-SA 3.0 |
| `blackbird.mp3` | *Turdus merula* | `Common Blackbird song (Turdus merula).ogg` | Diana Tudor | CC BY 4.0 |
| `swallow.mp3` | *Hirundo rustica* | `Hirundo rustica - Barn Swallow XC468712.mp3` | Marie-Lan Taÿ Pamart | CC BY-SA 4.0 |
| `wagtail.mp3` | *Motacilla flava* | `Motacilla flava - Western Yellow Wagtail XC436362.mp3` | Joost van Bruggen | CC BY-SA 4.0 |

Each file's Commons page carries the recordist's own description and the full
licence deed; five of the seven originate at xeno-canto and keep their XC
numbers in the filename so the original entry can be found.

**Changes made**, so that "indicate changes" is satisfied here and not only in
the abstract: one call, strophe or twitter was taken from each source by hand;
each was band-limited to the range its species actually occupies; 8 ms fades
were applied at both ends; and each was levelled to −1.0 dBFS peak and encoded
as mono mp3 at 16–22 kHz. The exact windows, filters and rates are the table at
the top of `tools/cut_birds.py`. At run time the game applies distance, stereo
placement, a lowpass standing for air, a pitch and rate walk down a series, and
the wall of the vikendica; none of that is baked into the files.

**Why CC BY-SA is not a problem here.** The obligations are attribution and
share-alike. Attribution is on the title screen (`veil.birds` in
`src/02-i18n.js`) and in full above and in `README.md`. Share-alike is already
what section 2 does: the assembled `flamme-retarde.html` is CC BY-SA 4.0, and
BY-SA 3.0 material may be adapted into a BY-SA 4.0 work under the one-way
compatibility the 4.0 deed grants. Nothing about these seven files changes the
terms on the rest of the bundle, and nothing in the bundle relicenses them —
they remain their recordists' work under their own deeds.

**Why Wikimedia Commons and not xeno-canto directly.** XC's API moved to v3 and
now requires a per-account key, which would put a credential in a build that
has to run from a clean clone. Commons mirrors much of the same catalogue with
the recordist and licence carried across and needs no key. It also settles the
licence question before it is asked: Commons does not accept non-commercial-only
files, so anything reachable through it is free for any use.

### The crowd's voices — synthesised, not recorded

`build/payload/bump_*_{m,f}.mp3` are thirty-two short lines — sixteen, in a
man's voice and a woman's — spoken by somebody you have walked into on the
promenade. They were generated with ElevenLabs text-to-speech on 23 August 2026
from wording written for this game, using two voices from that service's shared
library, `Pauly - Brooklyn Wise Guy` and `Brooklyn - African American New
Yorker`. No person said them and no recording of a person is in them.

The wording is mine and the licence above covers it. What ElevenLabs grants over
its own output is a matter between the account holder and ElevenLabs, and this
notice is here so that anyone redistributing the bundle knows which six files to
ask that question about. They are trimmed to the speech, levelled to -20 dBFS
RMS and cut to 24 kHz mono to sit with the field recordings.

### The radio — an off-air recording, and the one thing here I cannot license

`build/payload/radio.mp3` is 32.1 seconds cut by `tools/cut_field.py` from a
recording made on 23 August 2026 of a transistor set playing. The *recording*
is mine and the licence above covers it. What is coming out of the set is not:
it is a broadcast, and whoever wrote and recorded the music on it holds their
own rights in it, which no licence I grant can touch and which I am not
claiming to have cleared.

It is here because the three tunes it replaced were written out of oscillators
and read as a games console rather than as a station — see the note at the top
of the radio in `src/80-audio.js`. It is thirty-two seconds, band-limited by
the speaker it came out of and again by the doorway, and it plays only inside
one hut that you have to find. That is a judgement about proportion and not a
legal opinion, and anyone redistributing this bundle should make their own:
the clip is one file, it is named, and taking it out costs the radio its sound
and nothing else.

### Terrain heightfield — public domain

`build/payload/terrain_h.png` is resampled from
[AWS Terrain Tiles](https://registry.opendata.aws/terrain-tiles/) (Terrarium
encoding) via `tools/fetch_dem.py`. The underlying elevation for this tile comes
from public-domain sources (SRTM and successors). The Terrain Tiles dataset
aggregates several providers, some of which ask for attribution; the credit
above covers it.

### The intro panels — no grant offered

`build/payload/panel_*.webp` and `refs/panels/*.png` were generated with Google
Gemini 2.5 Flash Image from reference photographs, using `tools/gen_panels.py`.
They are deliberately **excluded from section 2**, because I am not in a
position to license them and would be lying if I pretended otherwise:

- They are machine output. Whether that attracts copyright at all is unsettled,
  and in at least some jurisdictions the answer is no.
- They were painted from reference photographs that are not mine — Wikimedia
  contributors, press and stock libraries. Those references are gitignored and
  have never been in this repository; see `refs/README.md`. The `rokici` panel in
  particular started from press photographs credited to **HVZ** and to
  **Hrvoje Jelavić / PIXSELL**.

They are included here as illustration for a non-commercial personal project. I
make no claim to them and offer no warranty as to their status. If you are
reusing this work — and especially if you are reusing it commercially — the
right move is to regenerate them: `tools/gen_panels.py` names the reference each
panel starts from, so you can substitute your own photographs, or drop the `ref`
argument and have the panel painted from the prompt alone. Rebuilding without
them costs a `python build.py`: `plate()` in `src/70-intro.js` returns early on a
missing key, so the cinematic still runs — narration, timing and sound unchanged
— over an empty stage.

If you hold rights in one of the source photographs and want a panel gone, open
an issue at https://github.com/unk1911/flamme-retarde/issues or write to
dolphin@edeliverables.com and it will come out.

---

## Building a version with less in it

One of the components above has terms someone might want to be clear of, and it
drops out cleanly, because the payload is a directory the build walks rather
than a manifest it hard-codes:

- **The panels.** Delete `build/payload/panel_*.webp` and rebuild, or regenerate
  them from your own references as above.

Nothing else is optional. Remove the OSM payload and there is no Šibenik, which
rather defeats the exercise.
